{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>For whoever eats fat of the animal from which one may offer</b> — it is impossible that its meaning should be 'of the animal that is itself an offering,' so as to exclude unconsecrated animals, for Scripture has already forbidden in the section of Vayikra (Leviticus 3:17) all fat without qualification, and there is there no condition or reservation. And here too it first forbade all fat of ox, sheep, and goat, and it forbade the fat of a nevelah (carcass of an animal that died on its own) and a terefah (animal torn or fatally flawed) under the prohibition of fat (Leviticus 7:24), and they are not offerings to HaShem. Moreover, it says there (Leviticus 3:17), 'an everlasting statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings,' and you will not find concerning any matter of the offerings the phrase 'in all your dwellings,' whereas concerning the gifts of the priesthood it says (Numbers 18:8) 'an everlasting statute' many times and does not mention 'in all your dwellings,' because the offerings are not an everlasting statute in all our dwellings but only in the Tabernacle and in the place that HaShem will choose. And let not your eyes be deceived by one who is confused by the verse 'And you shall eat neither bread, nor parched corn, nor fresh ears, until this very day, until you have brought the offering of your Elohim; it is a statute forever throughout your generations in all your dwellings' (Leviticus 23:14), for the new grain is forbidden by Torah law in every place (Orlah 3:9), for Scripture says that we may not eat bread, parched corn, or fresh ears forever, in all our dwellings, until the very day on which we bring the offering in the chosen House, and if there is no offering it is not forbidden from then on, for it did not say 'you shall not eat until you have brought the offering of your Elohim' as a permanent prohibition; rather, the prohibition is only until this very day alone. But its meaning is: until this very day when you will bring the offering, when you are able to bring it, and the reason is so that the offering may be a new meal-offering. And it is possible that 'the day' is drawn out — 'until this very day, until the day you bring the offering of your Elohim.' And the decisive proof that the meaning of 'of the animal from which one may offer' is 'of the species that is offered' is that thus it says concerning valuations (Leviticus 27:9), 'And if it is an animal from which one may offer an offering to YHWH, all that any man gives of it to YHWH shall be holy,' and its meaning is: an animal from whose species one may offer an offering to YHWH. And likewise, 'And if it is any unclean animal from which one may not offer an offering to YHWH' (ibid. v. 11) means plainly: of the unclean animal from whose species they do not offer. And there is no difference and no distinction between mentioning it in the plural, 'which they offer' and 'which they do not offer,' and saying it in the singular, 'which one may offer,' for its meaning is 'which a person may offer of it.' And likewise it says (Leviticus 11:39), 'And if an animal of which you may eat dies,' meaning the species that is eaten, not that the animal itself is eaten. And since it forbade all fat of the animal from whose species one brings the offering — not of the species that is not offered — and it might occur to one's mind to say that a nevelah and a terefah are considered a species from which one does not offer, it was necessary to mention that they are forbidden. And this is what is said (Leviticus 7:24), 'but you shall by no means eat it,' that they are subject to karet like all other fat, for the entire species that is offered is forbidden, even nevelot and terefot of it. And it says, 'it may be used for any work,' to say that it does not have the impurity of nevelot, for concerning nevelot it says, 'you shall not touch their carcasses' (ibid. v. 8), but concerning their fat it says that one may touch it and make any work from it. And if the prohibition of fat were only of offerings, why was it necessary to say concerning it, 'all fat you shall not eat,' there in the section of Vayikra (3:17), when He had already commanded that it be offered up on the altar for a pleasing fragrance — and how could we eat the fire of YHWH? And why did He not also warn concerning the lobe of the liver and the two kidneys of the offerings? And further, why was it necessary to mention (Leviticus 7:23) ox, sheep, and goat, for it is well known that one brings an offering only from them? And from the fact that Scripture says concerning disqualified consecrated animals (Deuteronomy 15:23), 'Only you shall not eat its blood,' and does not mention the fat — that matter will reach me in its proper place and I will explain it:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The final sentence (ועוד למה... ואפרשנו) refers forward to Ramban's discussion on Deuteronomy 15:23, where he addresses why the fat of disqualified consecrated animals is not mentioned there."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:53.706354417Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}